.lua
book.chapter(12, "Comprehensions and collection algorithms")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Comprehensions expose a search space"
.pp
A comprehension combines an output expression, generators, and optional
guards. It eagerly constructs a list. The notation is especially useful when
the result is naturally described by selecting combinations from finite
inputs. A generator binds its pattern for later generators, guards, and the
output expression. Source order determines nesting, so the final generator
varies fastest in a Cartesian product.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[squares := [x * x for x <- List.range 0 6 if x % 2 == 0]
pairs := [(x, y) for x <- [1, 2], y <- [10, 20]]
Test.equal "guarded output" squares [0, 4, 16]
Test.equal "nested order" pairs [(1, 10), (1, 20), (2, 10), (2, 20)]
present := [x for Some x <- [Some 1, None, Some 3]]
Test.equal "pattern selection" present [1, 3]]=], "Generators, guards, and pattern filtering")
.endlua
.pp
A generator pattern that does not match an element skips that element. This
differs from a failed function-parameter pattern, which reports a diagnostic.
The behavior is useful for selecting Some values, but it can hide discarded
errors if used casually with Results. Decide whether the application should
drop failures, stop at the first failure, or collect all failures before
choosing a comprehension as its main control structure.
.sh 2 "Translate notation into loops"
.pp
The nested-loop model predicts order and size. Two generators over lists of
lengths m and n can produce m times n results. A guard can reduce the output
but may still require examining the combinations that reach it. An earlier
generator's bindings can influence a later generator's source. This is more
expressive than simply zipping independent lists, and it can also allocate
far more than a reader expects from a short expression.
.lua
book.algorithm("Evaluate a two-generator comprehension", {
    "For each value x in the first source, in order:",
    {1, "Match the first generator pattern; skip mismatches."},
    {1, "Evaluate the second source in the environment containing x."},
    {1, "For each value y in that source, in order:"},
    {2, "Match the second pattern and evaluate applicable guards."},
    {2, "On success, evaluate the output expression and append its value."},
    "Return the accumulated list in encounter order."
})
.endlua
.pp
Keep generator and guard expressions pure when order is not part of the
intended public contract. An effectful guard turns a set-like description into
an ordered procedure with visible intermediate work. Sometimes that is exactly
what you need, but an explicit traversal then communicates the behavior more
clearly. A comprehension is not a promise of deferred or parallel evaluation.
.sh 2 "Map, filter, and folds"
.pp
List.map preserves one output position for each input position. Filter keeps
only elements satisfying a predicate. Filter_map combines optional production
with selection, and flat_map produces and concatenates zero or more outputs
per input. These operations differ in cardinality, which is often the most
important property when reviewing a transformation. A compiler pass that
must preserve declaration count should not silently use a dropping traversal.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[xs := [1, 2, 3, 4]
Test.equal "map" (List.map (* 2) xs) [2, 4, 6, 8]
Test.equal "filter" (List.filter (fn x -> x % 2 == 0) xs) [2, 4]
Test.equal "fold left" (List.fold_left (fn acc x -> acc - x) 0 [1, 2, 3]) (~6)
Test.equal "scan includes seed" (List.scan (+) 0 [1, 2, 3]) [0, 1, 3, 6]
Test.equal "empty scan" (List.scan (+) 0 []) [0]]=], "Traversal shape and accumulator order")
.endlua
.pp
A fold summarizes a list into an accumulator. The argument order of fold_left
is function, initial accumulator, list. Fold_right uses function, list,
initial accumulator. With noncommutative operations, direction and callback
argument order change the answer. A scan records intermediate accumulator
states and includes the seed, so its output has one more element than its
input. These details are contracts, not incidental implementation choices.
.sh 2 "Prefixes, searches, and partitions"
.pp
Take_while and drop_while describe a prefix boundary; filter examines the whole
input. Span returns both the longest satisfying prefix and the remaining
suffix. Partition also returns two lists, but groups every element according
to the predicate while preserving relative order within each group. Confusing
these operations is a common source of parsers that accidentally accept data
after the first invalid token.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[Test.equal "prefix boundary" (List.span (< 3) [1, 2, 3, 1]) ([1, 2], [3, 1])
Test.equal "whole partition" (List.partition (< 3) [1, 2, 3, 1]) ([1, 2, 1], [3])
Test.equal "first index" (List.find_index (> 2) [1, 3, 4]) (Some 1)
Test.equal "zip truncates" (List.zip_with (+) [1, 2] [10]) [11]
Test.equal "split count" (List.split_at 2 [1, 2, 3]) ([1, 2], [3])]=],
    "Similar names, different traversal contracts")
.endlua
.pp
Any, all, and find short-circuit. A callback after the decisive element is
not invoked. Partition, by contrast, examines the whole input. This distinction
affects both cost and effects. Prefer these semantic operations over writing
a full fold that computes unnecessary work, but do not assume an arbitrary
fold can short-circuit merely because its accumulator already contains the
final logical answer.
.sh 2 "Sorting and collection recipes"
.pp
Sorting is stable. List.sort_by expects a negative, zero, or positive comparator
result. Equal keys retain input order, which is useful when a second ordering
criterion is already encoded by the source sequence. The comparator must be
consistent; NaN-dependent comparisons and state-changing callbacks undermine
the intended ordering. Project records onto stable domain keys when that is
the equality the application actually needs.
.pp
The host List module adds windows, chunks, grouping, transpose, rotation,
seeded shuffle, and comparator-based extrema. Install it only when those
extensions are needed in an embedding. Windows can multiply the number of
stored elements, while uniq may be quadratic because it performs structural
membership checks. The shortest source expression is not always the best
algorithm for a large collection. Measure on representative element sizes,
including the cost of callbacks and comparisons.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
The portable contracts are in metalib/list/mod.ml; host extensions are in
metalib/cpp/List.h and its D counterpart. Build a small dependency report by
grouping edge pairs by their source node. First use comprehensions, then a
fold, and compare traversal counts. Add a test where a matching element occurs
after a failing prefix to distinguish span from partition. That single case
often exposes an incorrect mental model more effectively than many ordinary
sorted inputs.
